WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.688
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.064 --> 00:00:07.480
SAAD ALI: Hi, my
name is Saad Ali.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:08.980
I'm a software
engineer at Google,

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.680
and I work on the storage
stack of Kubernetes.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.810
Today, I'm going to talk
about storage classes

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:15.460
and dynamic volume
provisioning--

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.680
a new feature in Kubernetes.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.360
First I'll introduce
these concepts,

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.250
and then my colleague Matt will
walk you through a demo on GKE.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.460
Let's start by identifying
what problems Kubernetes volume

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.200
plug-ins are aiming to solve.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.050
First is that the basic unit
of scheduling in Kubernetes

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:34.080
is a pod, which is a
collection of containers.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.180
Containers do not share
their file system,

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:40.690
but pods are composed of
possibly multiple containers.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:43.260
This could be, for example, a
reader and a writer container

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.270
that work together.

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.610
So it would be really nice
to be able to share files

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.860
between containers in a pod.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.100
Second problem is that
containers are ephemeral,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.110
and the files inside
a container are also

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.030
ephemeral-- meaning
they're deleted when

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.500
the container is terminated.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.870
This means if a container
crashes or the machine

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:05.089
is restarted, you lose your
data inside the container.

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:06.880
If you're trying to
run a stateful service,

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.730
having your data
disappear in a nonstarter.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:12.490
This is where Kubernetes
Volumes come in.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:14.590
At its core, a volume
is just a directory--

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.650
possibly with some data
pre-populated in it--

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.740
which is accessible to all
the containers in a pod.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.290
How that directory
comes to be, the medium

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:27.430
that backs it, its content, what
happens to it after the pod is

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.610
terminated, are all determined
by the particular volume type

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.330
used.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:34.310
OK, let's take a look
at the volume plug-ins

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.510
we have in Kubernetes.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:40.080
The plug-ins can be categorized
into a few large buckets.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.450
One class of volumes
is persistent network

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.190
attached storage.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:47.370
These are volumes that outlived
the lifecycle of a pod.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.950
This could be anything from
manifests shared to a Cloud

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.900
provider virtual disk.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.430
The lifecycle of
these volumes is

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.730
independent from the lifecycle
of an individual pod.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:00.090
When a pod referencing one
of these volumes is created,

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.460
the storage is
automatically made available

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.740
inside the container
of that pod.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:08.107
The volumes are automatically
attached and mounted.

00:02:08.107 --> 00:02:09.690
When the pod is
terminated, the volume

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.700
is automatically unmounted
and detached if needed.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.820
But the data on the
volume remains intact--

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.440
and that's the key.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.090
The data is persisted.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.280
If another pod comes
along and references

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.620
the same instance of
the volume, they'll

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.100
see the same exact data.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.600
Another class of
volumes is ephemeral.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.020
These volumes are created when
a pod starts, and is deleted

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.280
when a pod is terminated.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.770
Empty dir is the
prime example here.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:38.400
It is shared scratch
space between pods.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.180
Kubernetes creates
an empty directory

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.150
on the underlying node machine,
either from the local disk

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.580
or from an in-memory tmpfs.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:49.420
And it mounts the directory into
all the containers in the pod.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:50.920
Then when the
containers start, they

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.380
have some shared scratch
space between them.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.870
When the pod is terminated,
the directory and its content

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:58.460
is deleted.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.830
Secret, ConfigMap
and DownwardAPI

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.530
are concepts in
the Kubernetes API.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.050
They are mechanisms for sharing
configuration and cluster

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:08.460
information with applications.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.220
One of the core
principles of Kubernetes

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.830
is to meet the users
where they are.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.710
This means that instead
of requiring applications

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:18.110
to talk to Kubernetes
API, we try

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:19.970
to make the data
available in the form

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.540
that applications already
know how to consume.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.600
So while existing
apps won't necessarily

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.790
know how to talk
to Kubernetes API,

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:31.930
they can probably already
read config info from a file.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.080
And so Secret, ConfigMap,
and DownwardAPI volumes

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.810
are a mechanism for exposing
these Kubernetes API

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.050
objects as files inside
the container of a pod.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.720
These volumes are essentially
an empty dir under the cover,

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.050
but pre-populated with the
contents of the Kubernetes API

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:51.140
server on creation,
instead of being empty.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.270
Now let's take a
look at an example.

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:54.520
Here we have a pod.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:58.040
A pod is a basic unit of
scheduling in Kubernetes.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.590
Pods are a collection of
containers, or in this case,

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.310
a single BusyBox container
that doesn't really do anything

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.110
but sleep on startup.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:09.050
This pod directly references
a Google Compute Engine

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:11.660
persistent disk volume
called Panda Disk for use

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:14.030
by all the containers
in this pod.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:17.040
The volume is assumed
to already exist.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.399
The pod also defines
where inside the container

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:21.470
the volume should be mounted.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:24.080
Now, this example
seems pretty trivial,

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:27.140
but the real beauty is what
happens under the covers.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.390
When the pod is created,
Kubernetes automatically

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:31.820
calls out to the
Google Cloud API

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:34.220
and attaches the
specified PD to the node

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:36.050
that the pod is scheduled to.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.570
It then formats the disk,
if needed, and mounts it

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:40.970
into the container
that makes up the pod.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:42.950
When the pod is moved
to another node,

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:45.890
Kubernetes automatically calls
out to the Google Cloud API,

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.680
detaches the persistent
disk from the first node,

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.960
and attaches it to
the second node.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.580
And repeats the
same song and dance

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.620
to mount the volume
inside the new instance

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.720
of the pod on the second node.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:59.001
All of this happens
invisibly for the user,

00:04:59.001 --> 00:05:00.500
and makes it so
that they don't have

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:04.490
to worry about what machine the
workload actually landed on.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.760
Kubernetes just
takes care of it.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:09.780
But there is something
not quite right here.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:12.260
Let's take a step back and
talk about a really nice design

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:15.530
principle that many of you
are probably familiar with--

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:18.030
the separation of concerns.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:21.060
It says that the components
managing distinct functionality

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.400
should, themselves,
be separated.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.460
This enables you to isolate,
revise, and test components

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:27.930
independently.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:30.210
It allows you to make your
larger system modular,

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.550
so that you can swap out
pieces without affecting

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:33.960
other components.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.190
And Kubernetes
does exactly that.

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:38.130
It takes functionality
that used to be

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:41.880
smeared across various layers
and handles them explicitly.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.560
It decouples the
application layer

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.220
from the cluster resources
required to run applications.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:49.300
That means, as an
application developer,

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.300
you don't have to worry
about the implementation

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.841
details of the cluster your
workload is running on anymore.

00:05:54.841 --> 00:05:57.090
You don't have to worry about
which particular machine

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.650
your app is running on.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:00.900
You don't have to worry about
whether that machine has

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.099
enough ports.

00:06:02.099 --> 00:06:04.140
You don't have to worry
about what other apps are

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:05.780
running on that machine.

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.280
Kubernetes abstracts
all of that away

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:11.430
and exposes an API that
allows you, as an application

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.820
developer, to simply describe
the service you want to run,

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:17.260
along with the resources
it will require.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.010
Kubernetes takes
care of the rest.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.120
If you think about it,
Kubernetes in this regard

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.300
is very much like an
operating system for clusters.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.850
Think back to the
earliest days of computers

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:30.160
when there were no
operating systems,

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.670
and every program needed a
full hardware spec to perform

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:35.860
even the basic tasks.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:37.930
Distributed system
application development

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:40.330
has been in this
stage for a long time.

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:43.570
And now, Kubernetes finally
introduces an abstraction layer

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:46.750
that hides the implementation
details and complexity

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:48.546
of cluster implementation.

00:06:48.546 --> 00:06:50.170
The real beauty of
an abstraction layer

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:51.670
is that it allows
you to swap out

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:53.500
the components underneath it.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:55.570
It enables portability.

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:58.300
You can run Kubernetes on a
lot of different environments.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:02.020
Everything from Cloud providers
like Google Cloud, AWS,

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:04.990
and even on Bare Metal
on-prem clusters-- all

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:07.030
with a consistent API.

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:09.250
This decoupling of
application from cluster

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:12.670
allows applications to be
Cloud provider agnostic,

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:15.340
and not worry about
network model, load

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:17.740
balancing, persistent storage.

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:19.930
This is great for you as
an application developer,

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:23.560
because it allows you to write
your applications, services,

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:25.690
deployment descriptions-- once.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.930
And have them work regardless
of the underlying cluster.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:31.270
It helps avoid vendor lock-in.

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:32.800
Now that's a really
pretty picture

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.650
I just painted, but for those
of you who are paying attention,

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:37.300
you probably are asking--

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:40.120
didn't your pod definition
specify exactly the type

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:43.000
of volume to use for
persistent storage?

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.850
You specified a GCEPD
with the name Panda Disk.

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:49.090
Now, if you take
that pod description

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.160
and drop it on a
non-GCE cluster,

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:52.930
it's not going to work, right?

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:54.670
You would be absolutely right.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:57.400
This is where the Kubernetes
concept of PersistentVolume

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:00.940
and PersistentVolumeClaim
objects comes into the picture.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:03.980
PersistentVolume and
PersistentVolumeClaim objects

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:07.090
enable an abstraction between
the consumption of storage

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:09.460
and the implementation
of storage.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:11.980
The PersistentVolume
object is a representation

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:14.980
of a piece of storage
available on the cluster,

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:17.410
and the PersistentVolumeClaim
is a request

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.660
for storage on behalf
of an application trying

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:21.470
to run on the cluster.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:23.470
Let's take a look at an example.

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:25.330
First, we have to
expose the storage

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.550
available for the
application to use.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:30.400
We do this by creating
a PV object to represent

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.280
each piece of storage
available for applications

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:34.840
to use on the cluster.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:36.909
Each PersistentVolume
object contains

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:39.250
a description of the
storage it exposes,

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:41.289
and connection information.

00:08:41.289 --> 00:08:43.450
Normally, cluster or
storage administrators

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:45.130
create these PV objects--

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:47.770
not application developers.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:49.980
Once these PV
objects are created,

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:52.280
they're available for use.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.130
Now if an application
developer wants

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.830
to use storage on this
cluster, they must first

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:59.260
create a request for storage.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:02.140
This is done by
creating a PVC object.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:05.050
In the PVC object, you define
the minimum requirements

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:06.520
for the storage you need--

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:10.480
the minimum disk capacity and
the required access modes.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:13.900
When the PVC object is created,
Kubernetes takes the request

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:17.170
and matches it with an
available, unbound PV.

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:20.140
In this case, it finds one of
the PV objects that we created

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:22.870
earlier-- a 100 gigabyte GCEPD--

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:24.760
is available and unbound.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.950
So it binds this
to the request--

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:28.150
the PVC.

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:32.160
Now this claim can be used
by the application developer.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.460
Let's go back to the pod
definition we used earlier.

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:37.320
In order to use the
storage, you can now

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.230
reference the claim for
storage, instead of directly

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.450
referencing a specific volume.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:46.530
The beauty of this is that our
pod definition is now portable.

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:48.330
The request for
storage resources

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:51.900
is decoupled from the specific
implementation of storage.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:54.900
We can take this pod definition
and drop it on any Kubernetes

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.720
cluster, and as long as
there is available storage,

00:09:57.720 --> 00:09:59.900
it will just work.

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:02.080
And even if you're running
only on a single cluster,

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:04.960
it means that your cluster
administrator can completely

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:08.260
swap out or modify the storage
system in your cluster,

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:11.510
but your pod definitions
won't need to change.

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.050
So the PersistentVolume
and PersistentVolumeClaim

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:17.159
abstraction is really nice
for giving us portability.

00:10:17.159 --> 00:10:19.450
But you probably noticed that
there was a step in there

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.580
that was a little cumbersome--

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:23.920
having a cluster admin
or storage provider

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.470
pre-provision all the
volumes available for use

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:27.770
in the cluster.

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:30.730
This is where dynamic volume
provisioning comes in.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:33.590
This is a feature that
is unique to Kubernetes.

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:36.670
The idea is that when a request
for storage is created--

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:38.560
when a PVC is created--

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:41.710
the cluster automatically
creates a new piece of storage

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:44.050
to fulfill that request.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:47.170
This makes a lot of sense in
Cloud environments like GKE,

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:49.570
where an API call can
provision new storage

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:51.960
within a matter of seconds.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:54.894
But even for non-Cloud
storage systems like NFS,

00:10:54.894 --> 00:10:56.310
you could write a
provisioner that

00:10:56.310 --> 00:11:00.360
maybe triggers the creation
of a new share on demand.

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:03.150
To use dynamic provisioning,
the cluster storage admin

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:05.040
must first enable it.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:08.620
They do this by creating one
or more StorageClass objects.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:10.620
The StorageClass
object specifies

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.140
the volume plugin to
use for provisioning,

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:15.990
and the parameters to
pass to that plug-in.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:17.870
The parameters for
the storage class

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:20.160
are a blob of key value pairs--

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:23.460
that the provisioner
presumably understands.

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:25.260
In this case, the
first storage class

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:29.520
says-- when executed, call
the GCEPD volume plug-in,

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:33.510
and pass in parameters to
create a standard spinning disk.

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:36.150
The second storage class pretty
much says the same thing,

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:40.770
but instead passes in parameters
to create an SSD-GCEPD.

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:43.560
The parameters are defined
by the volume plug-in

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.840
and set by cluster
admins, and are

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:48.840
opaque to Kubernetes otherwise.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:51.990
What's really neat about that
is that since the parameter

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:55.950
list is a blob that is passed
through to the provisioner,

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:57.960
Kubernetes doesn't have
to get into the business

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:01.680
of enumerating every possible
parameter for every storage

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:03.710
system in its API.

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:07.800
Plug-ins expose an arbitrary set
of knobs for disk provisioning,

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:11.110
and cluster admins can
tweak them at will.

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:13.930
Cluster admins can define
multiple storage classes

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:17.140
to expose different tiers, or
groupings, of storage cluster

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:18.130
users.

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:21.260
And users don't have to
worry about the details.

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:22.840
So in this case,
the cluster admin

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:26.140
exposed what they called
fast and slow storage.

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:28.030
And application
developers don't have

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:29.770
to worry about the
details of what

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:32.230
that means under the
covers, they just

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:35.290
pick from the storage classes
that are exposed to them.

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:37.900
To use storage-- just as
before-- the application

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:40.270
developer creates a
PersistentVolumeClaim

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:42.730
object to request storage.

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:44.620
In the PersistentVolumeClaim
object,

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:48.040
they now specify the
storage class to use.

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:52.930
In Kubernetes 1.6, this is a
new field in the PVC object.

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:57.040
In earlier versions, this was
an annotation on the PVC object.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:00.070
When a PVC object with a
storage class is created,

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:03.370
instead of binding to an
existing available PV,

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:05.440
Kubernetes calls the
provisioner defined

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:09.130
in the specified storage
class to create a new volume.

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:12.320
In this case, it calls
the GCEPD plug-in,

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:14.980
which calls out to
the GCE API to create

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:18.340
a new SSD-backed GCEPD.

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:20.650
Once the new storage
volume is provisioned,

00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:23.620
Kubernetes automatically
creates a PV object

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:26.110
to represent the new
storage, and then binds it

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:29.380
to the user's
PersistentVolumeClaim.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:32.260
It's important to note that
dynamically-provisioned volumes

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:35.500
are created with a
delete retention policy.

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:37.720
That means once
the PVC is deleted,

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:39.460
the dynamically-provisioned
volume

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:41.830
will automatically be deleted.

00:13:41.830 --> 00:13:43.520
If you don't want
that to happen,

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:45.610
you should update the
PersistentVolume object

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:47.800
after it is
automatically created,

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:51.640
and change the retention policy
to Retain instead of Delete.

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:54.820
Once the new volume is
created and bound to the PVC,

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:57.760
users can reference the PVC
in their pod definitions

00:13:57.760 --> 00:13:59.530
exactly the same way.

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:02.080
Something else to keep in
mind is that storage classes

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:05.260
can be marked as default.
Marking a storage

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:07.450
class as default
makes it so that even

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:10.300
a PVC without a
storage class specified

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:12.010
will trigger
provisioning, instead

00:14:12.010 --> 00:14:15.220
of binding to an
existing pool of PVs.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:17.170
This is a mechanism
for cluster admins

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:19.690
to force dynamic provisioning.

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:22.540
Also important to note
is that Kubernetes 1.6

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:24.940
will come with a storage
class pre-installed and set

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:28.570
as default for GKE and
other Cloud providers.

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:30.040
This is something
to pay attention

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:32.350
to if you've been using
Kubernetes for a while,

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:35.320
because it changes the default
behavior of PVC objects

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:37.450
on these Clouds.

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:40.450
It used to be that PVC objects
without storage classes

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:44.240
would bind to available
existing PV objects.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:47.290
But on these Clouds,
with 1.6, because there

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:49.630
will be pre-installed
default storage class,

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:51.820
a PVC without a storage
class will still

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:54.140
trigger the creation
of a new volume.

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:55.780
If you want to
disable this behavior,

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:57.490
you'll have to modify
the storage class

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:00.010
and remove the
default annotation.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:02.890
Now I'm going to hand it
over to Matt to demo this.

00:15:02.890 --> 00:15:04.360
MATTHEW DELIO: Thank you, Saad.

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:05.680
My name is Matthew DeLio.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:08.920
I'm a product manager
for Kubernetes and GKE,

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.070
and I work on
networking and storage.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:13.550
Today I'm going to be doing
a couple of demos for you.

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:18.100
The first is a demo of how
to manually provision and use

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:20.020
a disk inside GKE.

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:22.840
In the second, we'll use the
dynamic provisioning feature

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:24.740
that Saad talked about earlier.

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:26.140
So let's jump right in.

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:27.550
I've already created a cluster.

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:29.620
It has three nodes in
it, and each one of them

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:31.690
has one virtual CPU.

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:34.520
I'm using version
1.53 of Kubernetes,

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:37.600
so it's going to use the
annotation that Saad mentioned

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:40.340
earlier, rather than the
built-in storage class things

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:42.100
that we introduced in 1.6.

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:44.200
So going back to
my terminal screen,

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:46.030
I'm going to run this demo.

00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:49.900
And just to see that there
is no other disks inside GKE,

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:51.677
I'm going to run
this GCloud command.

00:15:51.677 --> 00:15:54.010
And from here, we can see
that there's just three disks.

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:55.630
These correspond
to the three nodes

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:57.392
that I have in my cluster.

00:15:57.392 --> 00:15:59.350
And now we're going to
check to make sure there

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:00.710
is no physical volumes--

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:03.610
PVs-- and there's also no PVCs--

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:06.200
Physical Volume Claims.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:08.630
Next, I will use
GCloud to create

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:11.870
a new disk inside
Google Cloud Platform,

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:14.360
and I will call
it Manual Disk 1.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:16.680
That's step number one.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:18.480
Next, we're going to
make sure it exists.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:20.146
So we're going to run
the GCloud command

00:16:20.146 --> 00:16:22.035
to list the disks that we have.

00:16:22.035 --> 00:16:24.330
And we can see that we
have our three node disks.

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:28.270
And now we also
have Manual Disk 1.

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:30.910
Next, we're going to add a
corresponding physical volume

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:32.290
to Kubernetes.

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:34.060
So I've just created
a simple YAML

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:38.230
file that lists the name
of the actual disk I

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:41.340
want to use-- that's the PV
name down at the bottom here.

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:43.300
And that says manual disk 1.

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:44.450
Then I can give it a name.

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:47.430
I'm going to call it
Manually Created PV.

00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:50.310
I have a capacity that's
set to 10 gigabytes,

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:53.921
and I've set the reclaim
policy to be Retain.

00:16:53.921 --> 00:16:55.670
So I'm going to load
that into Kubernetes.

00:16:58.480 --> 00:16:59.409
So that's in now.

00:16:59.409 --> 00:17:01.200
And now we're going to
make sure it exists.

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:03.834
So I'm running the
kubectl get pv.

00:17:03.834 --> 00:17:05.500
And that'll list the
disks that we have,

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:07.220
and we can see that it exists.

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:09.140
We have 10 gigabytes available.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:11.740
The reclaim policy is
what we expect it to be,

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:13.089
which is retain.

00:17:13.089 --> 00:17:16.256
And it's four seconds old.

00:17:16.256 --> 00:17:18.589
Now we're going to go ahead
and create a physical volume

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:23.630
claim by asking for 10
gigabytes of storage.

00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:27.380
We're going to call it My PVC,
and we're going to go ahead now

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:31.010
and load that into Kubernetes.

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:34.760
So at this point, we should have
a PV, and we should have a PVC,

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:38.020
and the PV should
be bound to the PVC.

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:40.490
So we're going to
check that real quick.

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:42.550
I've listed both the PVs--

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:44.350
the physical volumes--
and the PVCs--

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:46.105
the physical volume claims.

00:17:46.105 --> 00:17:47.480
Now in the first
couple of lines,

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:50.030
you see that we have a bound PV.

00:17:50.030 --> 00:17:55.970
It's bound to-- has a
claim of Demos/MyPVC.

00:17:55.970 --> 00:18:00.470
And the second last two
lines, we have My PVC,

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:02.780
and it's bound to
Manually Created PV.

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:09.560
The final step is to use this
physical volume claim in a pod.

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:11.340
And so here we are,
we've referenced

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:14.065
the claim name that's in
this line under volumes.

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:19.470
And we're going ahead
and creating a new pod.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:20.440
It's called Sleepy pod.

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:23.250
It doesn't do much, it
just sleeps for 100 minutes

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:24.360
and then goes to sleep.

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:30.010
So we go ahead and created that.

00:18:30.010 --> 00:18:33.100
It's going to take a few seconds
to warm up and actually create

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:33.735
the pod.

00:18:33.735 --> 00:18:35.110
And so while we're
waiting, we're

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:36.193
just going to check on it.

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:40.090
Looks like it's running now.

00:18:40.090 --> 00:18:41.631
So I'm going to go
ahead and show you

00:18:41.631 --> 00:18:44.140
what happens when you want to
tear down one of these setups.

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:49.400
So the first thing you need
to do is delete the pod.

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:53.480
At this point, you still
have a PVC in a PV.

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:57.090
And those are still
bound to each other.

00:18:57.090 --> 00:18:59.610
And while we're waiting
for that pod to delete--

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:02.070
it takes a few seconds
for the pod to delete.

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:05.270
But once it's done, we
should be able to see--

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:07.080
at this point our
pod is deleted.

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:09.800
Now we're going to go
ahead and delete the PVC,

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:11.225
and also delete the PV.

00:19:15.879 --> 00:19:16.670
So as you can see--

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:19.160
I just checked to make sure
that there's no PV and PVC

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:21.462
resources.

00:19:21.462 --> 00:19:23.920
And now we need to actually
delete the disk in Google Cloud

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:25.110
Platform.

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:29.240
So I ran the GCloud
command to do that.

00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:29.810
So that's it.

00:19:29.810 --> 00:19:32.870
There's four steps of manually
provisioning a physical volume

00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:34.474
and using it in Kubernetes.

00:19:34.474 --> 00:19:36.140
The first step is to
go ahead and create

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:38.030
the volume in Google Cloud.

00:19:38.030 --> 00:19:42.410
The second step is to create
a PV object in Kubernetes.

00:19:42.410 --> 00:19:44.400
Then you need to
create a PVC object--

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:46.110
a PhysicalVolumeClaim--

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:47.090
in Kubernetes.

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:49.550
And then you can
finally use it in a pod.

00:19:49.550 --> 00:19:52.970
Next I'll show how we can use
dynamic volume provisioning

00:19:52.970 --> 00:19:55.470
to make this process
a lot easier.

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:58.350
And so I'm going to go
ahead and start up my demo.

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:01.320
And just to show, again,
that there's nothing

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:05.100
going on behind the scenes,
I've got a list of all my disks.

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:08.430
We have-- again-- three nodes,
three disks corresponding

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:10.470
to those three nodes,
and no other disks

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:13.190
available in the system.

00:20:13.190 --> 00:20:14.750
Next I'm going to
go ahead and show

00:20:14.750 --> 00:20:16.833
that there's no storage
classes that we've already

00:20:16.833 --> 00:20:17.850
loaded into the system.

00:20:17.850 --> 00:20:19.419
As you can see, there's none.

00:20:19.419 --> 00:20:20.960
And finally, we're
going to make sure

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:23.585
that there's no physical volumes
or physical volume claims that

00:20:23.585 --> 00:20:25.100
exist in Kubernetes.

00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:27.770
So we have a pretty
vanilla system right here.

00:20:27.770 --> 00:20:29.510
We haven't done anything yet.

00:20:29.510 --> 00:20:32.860
The first step is to
add storage classes.

00:20:32.860 --> 00:20:35.840
And so, I've got
here is a YAML file.

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:38.570
It's very simple,
because I'm using 1.5.2--

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:40.280
which still is in beta.

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:42.260
But when it goes to
1.6, this will no longer

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:46.320
be a beta API object.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:47.960
I'm going to create
this storage class.

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:50.660
Its type name is Fast.

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:53.090
Its type is SSD.

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:55.460
And we're going to use
the GCE provisioner, which

00:20:55.460 --> 00:21:00.680
you can see is all listed
here in the YAML file.

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:03.159
So we'll go ahead and
load that into Kubernetes.

00:21:03.159 --> 00:21:05.575
Next we're going to go ahead
and list the storage classes.

00:21:05.575 --> 00:21:08.900
So you can see that we have
the Fast type of storage.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:12.710
And we can see that
it's a GCEPD type.

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:14.560
Now we just have to add a PVC.

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:16.670
So this one's a little
bit different than what

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:19.580
we had when we were manually
provisioning our storage.

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:21.170
This one has an annotation.

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:23.630
Again, the annotation
will be different

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:25.040
when we switch to 1.6.

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:27.350
But right now we're on 1.5.

00:21:27.350 --> 00:21:29.600
And I just have to add
the annotation to say--

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:32.670
I want this storage
class set to Fast.

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:35.310
And I say that I
want 10 gigabytes.

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:38.071
And once I create
this PVC, what it's

00:21:38.071 --> 00:21:39.570
going to do-- it's
going to go ahead

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:43.620
and provision the storage
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:47.640
And it's going to create a
bind to that storage object.

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:52.100
So to see that, we can go
ahead and list the disks.

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:54.050
And you can see that we
have a new disk here.

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:56.820
It's at the very
top of the list.

00:21:56.820 --> 00:21:58.910
It's a 10 gigabyte,
and its type is SSD--

00:21:58.910 --> 00:22:02.120
just as we expected when we
created this storage class.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:06.090
It's very simple to now use
the PVC in the pod.YAML file.

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:08.060
This is actually the
exact same YAML file

00:22:08.060 --> 00:22:12.830
I used earlier in the
manually provisioned example.

00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:15.910
So let's go ahead and
load that into Kubernetes.

00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:18.959
At this point, the disk
has already been created,

00:22:18.959 --> 00:22:20.500
and we just need to
start up the pod.

00:22:29.530 --> 00:22:31.930
Great, so now it's running.

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:34.760
We'll go ahead and just
show the deletion path.

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:36.610
So now I want to delete the pod.

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:39.554
Again, I just delete
the pod as before.

00:22:39.554 --> 00:22:40.970
It will take some
time for the pod

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:42.934
to actually shut
down and terminate.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:55.820
Now the pod is shut down.

00:22:55.820 --> 00:22:59.282
I'm going to go ahead and run
a GCloud compute disks list.

00:22:59.282 --> 00:23:00.740
And this will show
us all the disks

00:23:00.740 --> 00:23:02.420
that we still have
in our system.

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:04.160
As you can see
from here, we still

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:09.650
have a PVC-backed storage that
was dynamically provisioned.

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:12.770
And its size is still
10 gig, as we'd expect.

00:23:12.770 --> 00:23:15.050
I'm going to go ahead
and delete the PVC.

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:18.020
When I do that, I
would expect the PV--

00:23:18.020 --> 00:23:20.709
the actual backing
disk-- to be deleted.

00:23:20.709 --> 00:23:22.250
If we go ahead and
check that, now we

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:23.955
can see that it's
actually deleted.

00:23:23.955 --> 00:23:24.830
So there you have it.

00:23:24.830 --> 00:23:26.720
To use dynamic
volume provisioning,

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:29.150
all you have to do is
create a storage class,

00:23:29.150 --> 00:23:33.804
create a volume claim, and
then use the volume claim.

00:23:33.804 --> 00:23:35.970
And so as you can see,
dynamics storage provisioning

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:36.980
makes it a lot easier.

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:40.580
We only had to allocate
a storage class type,

00:23:40.580 --> 00:23:41.952
and we can use it everywhere.

00:23:41.952 --> 00:23:42.910
That's it for the demo.

00:23:42.910 --> 00:23:45.980
Thank you very much, and I'll
turn it back to Saad now.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:47.770
SAAD ALI: Thank you,
Matt, for that demo.

00:23:47.770 --> 00:23:50.290
So that's an overview of
dynamic provisioning and storage

